    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72380.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 9,
    "turn_text": "well my kids are big part of it the reason that i got interested in the tech issue is because of my children you know when my first boy was born when he was still pretty small we were struggling my wife and i figure out what what which we do in terms of technology do we let him have an iPad or mobile device a tablet or or not initially we said yes and then we We watched kind of how he was interacting with it and how he was getting really attached to it. We thought maybe this isn't such a great idea. Maybe we should learn more about this. So we went on this journey trying to figure out what is tech, how does it interact with kids, what's good for kids. We started talking to other families and we really learned that tech can be especially social media platforms can be really harmful to children, especially in large doses. It rewires their brains. There's an addictive element to social media, which is not happenstance. These companies have built their platforms to be addictive and for children, especially, you know, that can be a really, really scary thing with negative consequences. So that's really what got me into this issue. And then I was Attorney General of the State of Missouri. I opened an antitrust investigation against Facebook and against Google. And that really propelled me forward. It's become something I really care about. And I think it's the tech monopolies and the effect that they have on our speech, on our politics, and on our families is really what this book is about.",
