  {
    "episode_id": 72380,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72380.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 24,
    "turn_text": "My parents, I would say, were fairly strict disciplinarians and I think of myself. I try to be gentle with my kids in terms of I don't ever want to be overbearing. But we definitely believe in physical discipline when we're appropriate. The kids get older. I always think it's important the kids understand why it is they're being disciplined. So we often start with, I'll say, hey, I just told you to stop doing that. Do you understand why what you're doing is wrong? Do you understand why we don't hit brother in the face when we're angry? He takes her stuff and you just go punch him. Do you understand why that's not a good choice? Do you understand? We try to get them to develop that level of understanding. Then what I aim for is I want my kids to be able to have self-control. I think that you can try to hem them in with rules and don't do this and don't do that. Ultimately, you want the kid to have a sense of an internal direction where they say, I'm going to choose, to discipline my own emotions to discipline my own feelings to find a healthy outlet. So what I try to do and my wife and I try to do together very imperfectly is to try to give our kids a sense of, okay, self control where it's like I don't have to fly off the handle. I don't have to lose it right here. I can learn how to discipline my own emotions, control my own emotions, and to find healthy outlets for that. So, you know, we'll see.",
