  {
    "episode_id": 72380,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72380.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "Well, with the older boys, what I do is is that I usually we have dinner together as a family, and then what I'll do is I'll take the kids upstairs, give my wife a break, they'll go up to their room, they'll take their bath, take the shower, get into bed, and then I read to them, and I read to both of the boys together, and it's always constant battle as to how long we're going to read. It's not really a battle because I give in every time. I'll say, all right, we're going to read for 15 minutes, we'll get to the end of that, and I'll be like, how five more, daddy, five more. So we'll do that. We might also then wrestle a little bit. My boys love to play these games, like keep off the bed, so they'll try and push me off of the bed, and I'll try to keep them off. So we'll do that until my wife says, this is the opposite of bedtime. You're now getting them all riled up. So about the time we get called down, then we get them, actually get them into bed, and we say our prayers together at night, and then that's it, and put them to bed. And then with my daughter, of course, a little bit different story at six months. That's, you know, she's in charge of the house. So she gets to sleep whenever she wants to sleep. We don't really have her on a strict schedule, but it's just, you know, I'll hold her and sing to her a little bit and, you know, just tell her about my day. It's an incredible, incredible thing to hold that baby daughter and think that, man, her whole future, I can't wait to see what comes next.",
